---
import PlayerLayout from "../layouts/PlayerLayout.astro";
---

<style is:global>
  body { background: #050508; }
</style>

<PlayerLayout title="sleepcast — apple watch">
  <main class="mx-auto w-full max-w-2xl px-6 py-16 text-[#b59a76]">
    <a href="/" class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-[#6e5d44] hover:text-[#b59a76]">&larr; back to sleepcast</a>

    <h1 class="mt-8 text-2xl text-[#d9c3a0]">time your nights with an apple watch</h1>
    <p class="mt-2 text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-[#6e5d44]">an ios shortcut, built once</p>

    <section class="mt-10 space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        sleepcast guesses when you fell asleep from how still the phone went. your watch knows: it records sleep stages.
        this shortcut reads them from health and hands them to sleepcast, which then times your nights by the watch
        instead, and shows how far off its own guess was.
      </p>
      <p>
        sleepcast sends the data nowhere: it travels in the part of the link after the <code>#</code>, which browsers
        never send to a server, and stays in this browser. safari may keep the opened link in its history.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-8 text-base text-[#d9c3a0]">before you start</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
        <li>sleep tracking is on: in the watch app on your iphone, sleep, then track sleep with apple watch.</li>
        <li>you listen to sleepcast.pro in safari. a copy added to the home screen keeps its own storage, apart from safari's, so use the paste version below instead.</li>
        <li>your iphone is in english. sleepcast reads health's stage names (core, deep, rem, awake, in bed) in english for now.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="mt-8 text-base text-[#d9c3a0]">build the shortcut</h2>
      <p>in the shortcuts app, make a new shortcut called <em>sleepcast watch</em> and add these actions, in order:</p>
      <ol class="list-decimal space-y-3 pl-5">
        <li>
          <strong>adjust date</strong>: subtract <em>2 days</em> from the <em>current date</em>. this is where the
          shortcut's window opens.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>find health samples</strong>. set it to find health samples where <em>type</em> is <em>sleep analysis</em>,
          add a filter <em>end date</em> is after the <em>adjusted date</em> (end, not start: sleep already under way then is kept whole), add a filter <em>source</em> is your apple
          watch (so another sleep app's coarser samples don't blur the watch's), and sort by <em>start date</em>, oldest
          first.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>repeat with each</strong> item in <em>health samples</em>. inside the repeat, add a
          <strong>text</strong> action containing, with no spaces:
          <div class="mt-2 rounded border border-[#241f30] px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-[#d9c3a0]">start date~end date~value</div>
          <p class="mt-2">
            each of the three is the <em>repeat item</em> variable: tap it and pick its <em>start date</em>, <em>end date</em>
            or <em>value</em>. for the two dates, set the date format to <em>iso 8601</em> and turn on
            <em>include iso 8601 time</em>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li><strong>combine text</strong>: combine <em>repeat results</em> with <em>new lines</em>.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>text</strong>, on two lines: <span class="font-mono text-xs text-[#d9c3a0]">window~</span> followed by the
          <em>adjusted date</em> (iso 8601, time included, as above), then on the next line the <em>combined text</em>.
          the first line tells sleepcast where the window opens, so a night it cut into isn't misread.
        </li>
        <li><strong>url encode</strong> that <em>text</em>.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>text</strong>: <span class="font-mono text-xs text-[#d9c3a0]">https://sleepcast.pro/#watch=</span>
          followed straight away by the <em>url encoded text</em> variable.
        </li>
        <li><strong>open urls</strong>: the text from the step before.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        run it in the morning. sleepcast opens and says what the watch found, e.g. "your watch: asleep 12 min in;
        sleepcast guessed 20 min." the first time, health asks to let shortcuts read sleep data: allow it.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-8 text-base text-[#d9c3a0]">run it every morning</h2>
      <p>
        in shortcuts, automation, new automation, <em>time of day</em>, pick a time an hour or so after you usually get
        up, daily, then run <em>sleepcast watch</em> immediately. not on waking: the watch can take a while to hand the
        night's stages to your iphone, and too early it finds nothing yet. running it again later is safe: a night it has timed stays timed.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-8 text-base text-[#d9c3a0]">the paste version (home-screen app)</h2>
      <p>
        build the same shortcut, but in place of the last three actions add <strong>copy to clipboard</strong> with the
        two-line <em>text</em> (the window line, then the samples). run it, open sleepcast, go to <em>your rest</em>, then <em>paste from your watch</em>, and
        paste.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-8 text-base text-[#d9c3a0]">what it changes</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
        <li>a night is timed by the first stretch of sleep that begins inside it, within four hours of pressing start. sleep that had already begun when you pressed start doesn't count, nor do its later stages.</li>
        <li>only nights that began more than a minute after the window opened are timed: an earlier one may have been cut into, so it keeps the time it has.</li>
        <li>what was playing at that moment gets the credit in <em>what puts you under</em>.</li>
        <li>sleepcast keeps its own guess beside the watch's, and <em>your rest</em> shows how far apart they typically are.</li>
        <li>running it twice over the same nights changes nothing further.</li>
        <li>it closes any sleepcast night left open in another tab (the morning means the night is over), so run it after the night, not during one.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </main>
</PlayerLayout>
